   "source": [
    "### b. Scatter plot: Quantity and Price Correlation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 33,
   "id": "32a095e2-93c9-4755-b994-32515935db35",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Correlation between Quantity and Price:  -0.5163718700038966\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.figure()\n",
    "plt.scatter(df[\"Quantity\"], df[\"PricePerUnit\"])\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Quantity\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Price Per Unit\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Quantity vs Price Per Unit\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "correlation = df[\"Quantity\"].corr(df[\"PricePerUnit\"])\n",
    "print(\"Correlation between Quantity and Price: \", correlation)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9e20c0eb-ebb2-4a9f-92ec-4891386be444",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Findings:\n",
    "The correlation between Quantity and Price Per Unit is −0.52, which indicates a moderate negative correlation. This suggests that, in general, products sold in higher quantities tend to have lower prices, while higher-priced items are usually sold in smaller quantities. The scatter plot supports this observation, showing a downward trend as quantity increases. However, the relationship is not perfectly linear, implying that other factors may also influence sales volume."
   ]
  },
